Wear modelling for Class III (roller–shoe) friction couplings was performed for Tests X and XI (pure sliding couplings) using FEA and Archard’s wear model (Fig.  15 ). The agreement between simulated and measured wear volumes for Tests X and XI supports the applicability of Archard-based modelling to severe sliding regimes. Recent results reported in [ 59 ] indicate that wheel–rail wear cannot always be adequately characterized by a single global wear parameter, particularly when contact conditions vary along the wheel profile or when rolling–sliding regimes dominate. That study showed that indicators based solely on tangential energy dissipation may correlate with local wear depth but may not fully capture overall material removal when contact area and load distribution evolve. This observation is consistent with the present findings, where comparable friction levels can lead to different wear responses depending on creepage, contact pressure, and debris formation. It therefore supports the combined use of experimentally measured wear volumes and FEA–based stress analysis adopted in this work. However, deviations at lower slip may arise due to debris accumulation and transient lubrication mechanisms not captured by the model assumptions. The Archard wear model assumes that the volumetric material loss rate due to wear is proportional to the contact pressure and the relative sliding velocity at the contact interface. The wear is modelled according to Fig.  15  and can be described using Eq. ( 7 ) [ 54 ]: 7 Δ V = k H · P m · v rel n , \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
